{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789229547,"nonce":"J6IKJVo/cb/XiJOE031u17SHVAI=","message":"Fixed in fd63c6a. scripts/test_hot_reload.sh prints 'reloaded v2' and 'done v2' both interpreted and with ASH_ARGS='--jit-threshold 1', and now FAILS on 'reloaded v1'.\n\nWhat was wrong, in the order it was found:\n1. The SSA walker never polled the reload flag; only the opcode loop did, and the SSA walker is the default body. HLInterpreter::apply_pending_reload now runs after a call on both walkers.\n2. The recompile of a changed function was declined by the shared-module cost gate (\"Low ceiling is not worth the shared module (0 bodies)\"). JITModule::reload_recompile skips it: there is no correct fallback.\n3. Every changed findex went through one MCJIT module, which generates code once, so every function after the first failed get_function_address. One module per findex now.\n4. Only the ReloadContext's private mirror of functions_ptrs was patched; the live module_ctx table compiled callers load is written too.\n5. The walker inlined callees into the frame that polls (interpreter_config_for gave loop functions the standard, callees-visible key), so an inlined callee kept its V1 body for as long as the loop ran -- which also meant the script's \"reloaded v2\" came from init_constants re-seeding the string global, not from calling getMessage. Under --hot-reload every walker body is now AirConfigKey::interpreter() (no inlining, hence no OSR); with ASH_AIR_LOG=1 the recompiled findex is re-prepared after the reload line.\n6. self.air.invalidate() pulled the optimized body out from under a running opcode frame (interpret_loop re-resolved air.body() every iteration; the raw body numbers pcs differently, so under ASH_AIR=v2-serialize the loop restarted with a fresh deadline and never printed). InterpreterFrame.body pins the body for the frame's life.\n7. compute_function_hashes hashed Int/Float literals by pool index, so a numeric-literal-only change was not a changed function; it hashes literal content and constant globals now.\n8. hlp_gc_walk_heap took used_blocks (byte offsets) for addresses and panicked on the first reload whose diff changed a constant field.\n\nWhat the script proves: detection, recompile ([tier] body findex=N (shared) under ASH_TIER_LOG=1), the changed body re-prepared and executed by the interpreter in both arms; the review's probes (two changed functions with a field patch, a callee inlined into the polling loop, a numeric-literal change) print the V2 values.\n\nWhat it does not prove: a COMPILED caller reaching the recompiled body -- main stays interpreted in the fixture, and a reload clears every tiered entry anyway. Still open, ada0aa8: the brokers keep the startup bytecode, so a promotion of a changed findex that is in flight when the reload lands, or a later re-promotion, installs the V1 body over the V2 one. Also open: hlp_gc_walk_heap visits one object per line, so line-shared small objects after the first are not reached by field patches.","files":null}]}